body:
i've been trying for almost a month on this thread https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/cake-php/Tuz9T7hO3eg to get a clear answer, but haven't made much progress. i'm hoping by trying it here, some discussion can help clarify if i'm doing something wrong, or there's an untested scenario & possible bug in the ORM.

@markstory was on the IRC channel last week & there was a brief conversation about saving a primary record with a BTM association, and `_joinData` in the middle. The documentation & tests provide these scenarios:
1. When you're saving the primary record (`Articles`, for instance) you're also saving new `Tags`, and adding `_joinData` (`rating`, maybe a nested association to `User`, etc.). 
2. You can retrieve an _existing_ `Articles` entity and patch it plus the association `Tags` and the `_joinData`.

Per the discussion last week with Mark, there might be something missing for this scenario:

i have `Tags` already saved. i don't want to change any of them or create new when i work on `Articles`. i want to pick from the existing `Tags`, and save some `_joinData` in the pivot table. All i should need from `Tags` is the ID so i can create the linking record with `article_id`, `tag_id`, and the additional data.

My POST data looks like the documentation says it should:

``` php
[
// ... Some fields for the primary Article entity, then...
    tags => [
        3 => [ // This is the form input index for Tags, not an ID
            id => 1, // The Tag ID
            _joinData => [
                value => Save me! // Yes, value is the column name
            ]
        ]
    ]
]
```

In my Controller:

``` php
$article = $this->Articles->newEntity();
if ($this->request->is('post')) {
    $article = $this->Articles->patchEntity($article, $this->request->data, 
                                            ['associated' => ['Tags._joinData']]);
    if ($this->Articles->save($article)) {
        // rejoice!
    }
}
```

The patched Entity looks like:

``` json
{
    "...": "Fields for the primary Article entity, then...",
    "tags": [
        {
            "id": 1,
            "...": "Other fields for the Tags entity"
        }
    ]
}
```

i Logged `$article->errors()` on the patched entity and got NOTHING, so everything checks out, right? But where is the `_joinData`???

After the save _SUCCEEDS_, the updated entity looks like:

``` json
{
    "...": "Fields for the Article entity, then...",
    "tags": [
        {
            "id": 1, 
            "...": "Other fields for Tag entity, then...",
            "_joinData": {
                "id": 13,
                "article_id": 14,
                "tag_id": 1
             }
        }
    ]
}
```

Wait, WHAT? Yes, the `Tags` entity is now looking up the existing Tag (thanks to PR #6086, i think), and it's generating the linking record (`_joinData`). But because `value` wasn't preserved during `patchEntity()`, the column in that record is `NULL`.

Table classes (per http://book.cakephp.org/3.0/en/orm/associations.html#using-the-through-option):

``` php
class ArticleTable extends Table {
    public function initialize(array $config) {
        // ... displayField, addBehavior, other relationships...
        $this->belongsToMany('Tags', [
            'through' => 'ArticlesTags'
        ]);
    }
    public function validationDefault(Validator $validator) {
        // Everything is exactly as the bake command wrote it.
    }
    public function buildRules(RulesChecker $rules) {
        // Everything is exactly as the bake command wrote it.
    }
}

class TagsTable extends Table {
    public function initialize(array $config) {
        // table, primaryKey, displayField, etc...
        $this->belongsToMany('Articles', [
            'through' => 'ArticlesTags'
        ]); 
    }
    public function validationDefault(Validator $validator) {
        // All validation commented out, per discussion with @markstory
    }
}

class ArticleTagsTable extends Table {
    public function initialize(array $config) {
        $this->belongsTo('Articles');
        $this->belongsTo('Tags');
    }
    public function validationDefault(Validator $validator) {
        $validator
        // Typical validation as written by bake, then...
            ->requirePresence('value', 'create')
            ->allowEmpty('value')
            ->allowEmpty('article_id', 'create')
            ->allowEmpty('tag_id', 'create')
    }
    public function buildRules(RulesChecker $rules) {
        $rules->add($rules->existsIn(['article_id'], 'Articles'));
        $rules->add($rules->existsIn(['tag_id'], 'Tags'));
        return $rules;
    }
}
```

i _hoped_ PR #6086 would address this problem, but after updating to the current HEAD, it's not happening. i'm stumped. i managed to get a different approach to work, using `$this->Articles->Tags->link()`, but i listed several reasons i really want to avoid using that in the Google Group thread.

Can someone help? i haven't managed to be on IRC when Mark is around to explore this further, and if it's a bug, it would be great to get a fix for it. If it's something i didn't do correctly to my Table or Entity classes, i'll make changes, but i suggest the issue stands as a documentation problem, since i've left those classes virtually untouched since **bake** wrote them. Changes i made were to use the `'through'` option (per docs), and disabling validations as requested during conversation.

Thanks everyone.
